content.text:
Here's a realistic end-to-end example — a little order-notification bot. The scenario where templates earn their keep: your customer messaged you Tuesday, their order ships Thursday, and now you need to tell them — but the 24h window is long gone.

```ts
import { Spectrum, text } from "spectrum-ts";
import {
  whatsappBusiness,
  whatsappTemplate,
} from "@spectrum-ts/whatsapp-business";

const app = await Spectrum({
  projectId: process.env.PHOTON_PROJECT_ID,
  projectSecret: process.env.PHOTON_TOKEN,
  providers: [whatsappBusiness.config()],
});

// ── Thursday: order ships, conversation is 2 days stale ──────────────
// A free-form send here would fail (Meta error 131047: outside the
// customer-service window). A template is the ONLY thing Meta accepts.
//
// Prereq (one-time, in Meta's WhatsApp Manager): an approved template, e.g.
//   name: "order_shipped", language: en_US
//   body: "Hi {{1}}! Order {{2}} shipped and arrives {{3}}."

const space = await app.space("15551234567"); // customer's number

await space.send(
  whatsappTemplate({
    name: "order_shipped",
    languageCode: "en_US",
    bodyParams: ["Jessica", "#1042", "Saturday"], // fills {{1}} {{2}} {{3}}
  })
);
```

Customer receives: *"Hi Jessica! Order #1042 shipped and arrives Saturday."* — and here's the payoff: **the moment they reply, the 24h window reopens**, and you're back to normal free-form conversation:

```ts
for await (const [space, message] of app.messages) {
  // Their reply reopened the window — plain sends work again.
  await space.send(text("It's with the courier now — anything else? 📦"));

  // Bonus from fix #1: if they quote-replied your template
  // ("when exactly on Saturday?" quoting the shipping message),
  // you now actually see that relationship:
  if (message.content.type === "reply") {
    console.log("customer is asking about message:", message.content.target.id);
  }
}
```

A second common pattern — **try free-form first, fall back to template** when you don't track window state yourself:

```ts
try {
  await space.send(text("Your order shipped!"));
} catch (err) {
  // 131047 = outside the 24h window → only a template will go through
  await space.send(
    whatsappTemplate({ name: "order_shipped_generic", languageCode: "en_US" })
    // no bodyParams → template has no {{variables}}, sends as approved
  );
}
```

Three things worth knowing when you use it for real:

1. **The template must pre-exist and be approved** in WhatsApp Manager. This code doesn't (and can't) create templates — if the name doesn't exist or isn't approved for that language, Meta rejects with 132001 at send time.
2. **Param count must match the approved body.** `order_shipped` declares three `{{n}}` slots, so `bodyParams` needs exactly three strings, or Meta rejects with 132000. We can't validate that client-side because only Meta knows the template's definition.
3. **On any other platform it degrades safely.** If this same code ran against a space on another provider, the `__platform: "WhatsApp Business"` tag makes the framework warn-and-skip the template send instead of crashing — same mechanism as iMessage's mini-app content going the other way.

The nice property of the design: nothing about `space.send(...)` changed. Templates ride the exact same call apps already use — the provider just recognizes its own content type on the way through via `isWhatsAppTemplate` and routes it to the SDK's template channel instead of the text one.

